# .devcontainer/sway-config -- passed to sway with `-c` by start-wayland.sh.
#
# A compositor with no screen, no seat and no keybindings. This file replaces sway's distributed
# default outright, so everything the headless session needs has to be stated here; nothing is
# inherited.

# One virtual screen. Comfortably larger than the app's 1000x700 window -- the app's zoom scales
# what it draws, not the window -- so the window is never clipped and never as large as the output
# it sits on.
output HEADLESS-1 resolution 1920x1200

# Every window keeps the size it asked for. sway tiles by default, which would stretch the app to
# fill the output and lay it out at a width it never has on a desktop. That is the one difference
# of the lot that would change what the app itself draws rather than what surrounds it, since egui
# reflows to the window it is given.
for_window [app_id=".*"] floating enable

# No frame. A frame belongs to the compositor, so any sway drew would differ from the desktop's
# without saying anything about the app. With none, a screenshot is the app and nothing else.
default_border none

# winit binds to whichever display server it can reach, and an X11 path is exactly the difference
# this container exists to remove. Without Xwayland, a failure to reach Wayland is an error that
# names itself instead of a quiet success over a protocol the desktop is not using.
xwayland disable

# A keyboard that exists for the whole session. Without one the seat has no devices at all -- there
# is no hardware here -- and an app that starts on a seat with no keyboard never binds one. Each
# `wtype` call then creates a virtual keyboard, types, and destroys it within milliseconds, before
# the app has a keyboard to receive the keys on, so every keystroke vanishes without an error.
#
# `wtype -s` holds its keyboard open while it sleeps, and sleeping is all this one does. It sleeps a
# day at a time in a loop because the argument is a millisecond count with a ceiling, and a
# container can outlive any single sleep. The loop runs only while `wtype` succeeds: once the
# compositor is gone every call fails at once, and a loop that ignored that would spin. A `wtype`
# already asleep when sway stops does not notice until it wakes, so a sway restarted by hand leaves
# an idle loop behind for up to a day; stopping the container ends everything.
#
# Started by sway rather than by start-wayland.sh, so it lives exactly as long as the compositor
# whose seat it sits on, and in the compositor's own session -- not in the session of a startup
# script whose processes the container's tooling is free to stop once it returns. It is running
# before any app can connect, which is the point of it.
exec sh -c 'while wtype -s 86400000; do :; done'

# A pointer that exists for the whole session, for the same reason and started the same way. The
# packaged tools cannot hold one open -- `wlrctl` creates a pointer per call and destroys it -- so
# virtual-pointer.py does, and it ends when the compositor does. With it on the seat,
# `swaymsg seat seat0 cursor set <x> <y>` and `cursor press|release button1` move and click;
# `virtual-pointer.py wheel <n>` scrolls. start-wayland.sh passes the script's directory, since this
# file does not know where it was read from.
exec python3 "$DEVCONTAINER_DIR/virtual-pointer.py"
